result.design:
Questions answered: What constitutes a unit? Which differences require separation? Where does each unit live? How is it named? Which material may be edited? Who owns it? What causes it to change? How is it delivered and supported? Which dependencies are permitted? Which checks apply?

Unit definition: a unit is the smallest cohesive, independently enforceable workspace component with one public surface, human owner, capability, provenance class, licence class, lifecycle and execution contract. Every unit has a package.json with estate.unit set to true, even when it contains only data or generated assets. Direct cross-unit relative imports are forbidden; dependencies use package names and declared exports. A nested directory is not another unit unless it has its own manifest.

Mandatory split conditions: create separate units whenever licence class, authorship/editability, delivery or support promise, refresh trigger or mechanism, human ownership, credential boundary, execution consumer, or failure contract differs. An independently consumed generated or built artefact is also a unit. Extract generic machinery from a product unit when it has a coherent API and can be used without product concepts. Sharing an external service, visual structure, commit, repository, or broad subject does not justify merging units. Size alone does not justify splitting a cohesive library.

Layout:
products/<domain>/<capability> for supported deployables and CLIs;
demos/<domain>/<capability> for illustrative applications;
packages/foundation/<capability>, packages/security/<capability>, packages/<domain>/<capability>, and packages/integrations/<commodity>/<capability> for reusable code;
tooling/commands/<capability>, tooling/guards/<capability>, tooling/validators/<capability>, tooling/generators/<capability>, and tooling/jobs/<capability> for repository automation;
studies/<study>/<capability> for quality-gated, non-shipping evidence code;
ops/<system>/<action> for live-system operations;
materials/external/<domain>/<feed>, materials/generated/<domain>/<artefact>, materials/curated/<domain>/<dataset>, materials/vendor/<vendor>/<bundle>, and materials/brand/<brand>/<asset-set> for non-code or immutable material.

Paths and package names use lower-case kebab-case capability nouns, never catch-all names such as common, shared or utils. Package identifiers use @estate/<domain>-<capability>; directory kind is omitted from the identifier so a support-policy move need not rename imports. Public packages may receive an explicitly governed public scope and name. Executable and binary names are declared separately.

The estate manifest block records kind, domain, capability, scope, support, delivery, authority, licenceClass, owner, lifecycle, execution and riskFlags. lifecycle records trigger, mechanism and producer; measured cadence is operational metadata rather than identity. execution records consumer class, failure mode and permitted secret references. The single curriculum credential may be referenced only by composition roots such as products, jobs or ops; libraries accept credentials through typed injection and cannot read ambient secrets.

Enforcement: a repository unit linter validates paths, names, manifests, ownership and homogeneous licences; a dependency-boundary rule rejects undeclared cross-unit imports and forbidden graph directions; exports restrict public APIs; generated and vendored trees are checksum-protected and reproducibility-checked; affected-unit CI is calculated from the dependency graph; assurance gates are derived from kind, delivery and risk flags. Product and security-critical units receive the strongest gates, demos receive explicit reduced support, studies receive product-level code-quality gates without delivery gates, and ops receive operational safeguards such as environment confirmation, dry-run support and audit logging rather than product gates. Large material may be stored directly or through a content-addressed descriptor without changing its unit boundary.

Organise around independently buildable, owned units with one dominant contract: a deployable surface, a public/reusable library, a workflow adapter, a generated/data boundary, or an asset/content boundary. Never let directory proximity, implementation language, or an external-service proper noun decide unit membership. Split when ownership, licence, release/support promise, consumer failure mode, generation trigger, or public API differs; keep together when code changes atomically behind one contract and is consumed/released together.

Layout:
apps/{mcp-server,search-cli,product-*,demo-*}
packages/{foundation,security,graph,client-curriculum,search-retrieval,design-tokens,observability-*}
tooling/{shared-policy,commit-hooks,repo-validators,interactive-cli,scheduled-jobs,live-operations}
integrations/curriculum/{spec-snapshot,bulk-snapshot,types-generator,bulk-schema-generator,corpus-miner,curated-content}
generated/{spec-types,search-contracts,bulk-schemas,widget-dist,design-token-css}
widgets/{interactive-widget-source}
content/{reference-data}
assets/{brand}
studies/{research-evidence}

Each top-level directory is a routing category, not an API boundary by itself. Every leaf unit has its own package manifest, explicit owner, licence declaration, dependency declaration, and test/build contract. Generated outputs are either separately packaged immutable deliverables or omitted from source control; their manifest records generator, source unit, event trigger, and reproducibility check.

